# SQL Server

## Mục tiêu

SQL Server là hệ quản trị cơ sở dữ liệu quan hệ của Microsoft, được sử dụng rộng rãi trong các doanh nghiệp, đặc biệt trong môi trường Windows và các ứng dụng .NET. Trong Cursor Enterprise Framework, SQL Server đóng vai trò là lựa chọn cho các tổ chức đã đầu tư vào hệ sinh thái Microsoft hoặc cần các tính năng enterprise như Advanced Analytics, Always On Availability Groups, và deep integration với other Microsoft products.

Mục tiêu của rule này là cung cấp hướng dẫn chuyên sâu cho việc thiết kế, phát triển, và vận hành SQL Server databases. Các nguyên tắc cover T-SQL programming best practices, indexing strategies specific to SQL Server, configuration tuning, security implementation, và operational procedures. SQL Server có nhiều features đặc thù như CTE syntax, window functions, temporal tables, và columnstore indexes mà developers cần nắm vững.

## Nguyên tắc

**1. Object Naming Conventions**

Table names: PascalCase, singular nouns (Customer, Order, Product). Column names: PascalCase, descriptive. Stored procedures: usp_<Entity>_<Action> pattern. Functions: fn_<Purpose> pattern. Views: vw_<Description> pattern. Indexes: IX_<Table>_<Columns> pattern. Constraints: CK_<Table>_<Description>, DF_<Table>_<Column>. Always use two-part names: schema.object (dbo.Customer).

**2. Data Type Selection**

Use appropriate data types: INT for integers, BIGINT for large values. VARCHAR(MAX) for large text, not TEXT. NVARCHAR(MAX) for Unicode large text. DECIMAL(p,s) for precise decimals, FLOAT for approximate. DATETIME2 for new development (more precise). DATE, TIME for date-only or time-only. ROWVERSION for concurrency control. UNIQUEIDENTIFIER for GUIDs with NEWSEQUENTIALID() default.

**3. Schema Design**

Always use schemas for logical grouping (dbo, sales, inventory). Keep schemas consistent across database. Default to Third Normal Form, denormalize based on performance. Primary keys: INT IDENTITY for small tables, BIGINT for large. Foreign keys enforce referential integrity. CHECK constraints for business rules. DEFAULT constraints for common values.

**4. Indexing Strategy**

Clustered index on primary key (usually). Non-clustered indexes for query optimization. Covering indexes with INCLUDE for specific queries. Filtered indexes for subsets of data. Columnstore indexes for analytical workloads. Indexes on foreign key columns. Avoid over-indexing on write-heavy tables. Monitor index usage and remove unused indexes.

**5. T-SQL Best Practices**

Use SET NOCOUNT ON in stored procedures. Always qualify column names in joins. Use ANSI JOIN syntax (INNER/LEFT/RIGHT JOIN). Avoid SELECT *, specify columns. Use TRY...CATCH for error handling. Use transactions for multiple related changes. Implement TRY_CONVERT/TRY_CAST for safe casting. Use OFFSET/FETCH for pagination.

**6. Stored Procedures Guidelines**

One purpose per stored procedure. Use input/output parameters properly. Return single result set when possible. Implement error handling and logging. Keep procedural logic minimal, use set-based operations. Encrypt procedures containing sensitive logic. Document with extended properties.

**7. Performance Tuning**

Use execution plans (Actual vs Estimated). Update statistics regularly. Rebuild/reorganize indexes based on fragmentation. Monitor wait statistics. Use query store for troubleshooting. Parameter sniffing awareness. Avoid cursor-based solutions when possible. Use set-based operations.

**8. Security Implementation**

Use contained databases for portability. Windows authentication when possible. Principle of least privilege. Schemas as security boundaries. Encrypt sensitive columns (Always Encrypted). Dynamic data masking for PII. Row-level security for multi-tenant. Audit sensitive operations.

**9. Backup Strategy**

Full backup daily minimum. Differential backups every few hours. Transaction log backups every 15 minutes. Test restore procedures regularly. Store backups on separate storage. Consider backup compression. Use CHECKSUM for backup validation. Azure Blob Storage for cloud backup.

**10. High Availability**

Always On Availability Groups for enterprise HA. Failover clustering for instance-level HA. Database mirroring deprecated, use AGs. Log shipping for disaster recovery. Read-scaleout with readable secondaries. Automatic page repair. Cross-database referencing carefully.

